🌐🔒 Public vs Private IP Address — What’s the Difference?

In networking, public and private IPs serve different purposes — and understanding them is key to managing secure and efficient systems.

🧭 Private IP (Local/Internal):
🏠 Used by devices inside your home or office network
📶 Assigned automatically by your router
🔐 Can’t be accessed from the internet directly
📲 Found in device settings, typically used for LAN communication

🌍 Public IP (External):
🌐 Assigned by your Internet Service Provider (ISP)
🔓 Used to communicate with the outside world (internet)
📍 This is what websites and apps see when you go online
🔍 Can be found by Googling “What’s my IP”

⚙️ How They Work Together:
Your router acts like a middleman — using NAT (Network Address Translation) to map internal (private) IPs to your public IP for internet access, and vice versa.

🚫 Why It Matters:
Confusing public and private IPs can lead to connectivity issues, security risks, and troubleshooting headaches.
Understanding this split is foundational for network engineers, IT admins, and even everyday users.
